---
import CodeBlock from '../components/site/CodeBlock.astro';
import Section from '../components/site/Section.astro';
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { RELEASE } from '../data/landing.ts';
import {
  BACKUP,
  CLI,
  COMPOSE,
  DEMO,
  INSTALL_BLOCK,
  INSTALL_FLAGS,
  SIZING,
  UPGRADE,
} from '../data/self-hosting.ts';
import { REPO_URL } from '../lib/links.ts';
---

<Page
  title="Self-hosting"
  description="Run Bemmoly on one VM with one command, or with Docker Compose, Helm, Terraform or an air-gapped bundle. Upgrades, rollback and backups are built in."
  lede="Bemmoly is one container next to one Postgres. One command sets it up on a fresh VM, and the same image runs under Docker Compose, on Kubernetes and in networks with no internet access."
>
  <p class="rounded-card border border-br bg-sf2 px-4 dark:bg-sf py-3 text-14h leading-desc text-tx3">
    Version {RELEASE.version} is still being built. This page describes how it installs; the
    installer and images are published with the release. Follow progress on
    <a href={REPO_URL}>GitHub</a>.
  </p>

  <Section title="Install with one command" id="install">
    <CodeBlock code={INSTALL_BLOCK} label="On a fresh VM, as root or with sudo" />
    <p>The installer checks the machine first and prints the fix for anything missing. Then it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>installs Docker and the Compose plugin if they are not there;</li>
      <li>asks for your domain, and uses a temporary certificate until DNS points at the VM;</li>
      <li>
        writes everything to <code>/var/bemmoly</code>: the Compose file, the proxy config, an
        <code>.env</code> with generated secrets, and folders for data and backups;
      </li>
      <li>starts the stack, waits for Postgres, applies the schema and prints the URL.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Open the URL to run the six-step setup wizard and create the first admin. Flags skip the
      questions or leave parts out:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={INSTALL_FLAGS} />
  </Section>

  <Section title="Requirements" id="requirements">
    <ul>
      <li>Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora or Amazon Linux, with root or sudo.</li>
      <li>2 GB of memory and 10 GB of disk at minimum.</li>
      <li>Ports 80 and 443 free and reachable, and outbound DNS.</li>
      <li>Postgres 18 is installed for you. To bring your own, see Docker Compose below.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-card border border-br">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-14">
        <thead class="bg-sf2 dark:bg-sf text-12 font-semibold tracking-caps text-tx4 uppercase">
          <tr>
            <th class="px-3.5 py-2.5 font-semibold">Team</th>
            <th class="px-3.5 py-2.5 font-semibold">VM</th>
            <th class="px-3.5 py-2.5 font-semibold">What to expect</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {SIZING.map((row) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-br-row">
              <td class="px-3.5 py-2.5 font-medium text-tx">{row.team}</td>
              <td class="px-3.5 py-2.5 whitespace-nowrap">{row.vm}</td>
              <td class="px-3.5 py-2.5">{row.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Docker Compose" id="compose">
    <p>
      The installer writes a plain Compose file, so you can also run it by hand. Four services, two
      of them optional:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={COMPOSE} label="/var/bemmoly/docker-compose.yml (shortened)" />
    <p>
      To use a Postgres you already run, set <code>DATABASE_URL</code> in <code>.env</code> and drop
      the <code>db</code> service. Postgres 18 is recommended; 17 works with a warning. Without the
      pgvector extension everything works except semantic search, and the health page says so.
    </p>
    <p>To look around on your laptop first, the demo mode starts with sample data:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={DEMO} />
  </Section>

  <Section title="Kubernetes with Helm" id="helm">
    <p>
      The Helm chart runs the same image as separate api and worker deployments with autoscaling,
      stores files on a volume or in an S3-compatible bucket, connects to your Postgres or brings
      one as a subchart, and sets up Ingress with cert-manager. When an update is available, the
      Updates page shows the exact <code>helm upgrade</code> command.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Terraform" id="terraform">
    <p>
      Modules for AWS (EC2 with RDS), GCP (Compute Engine with Cloud SQL) and Hetzner (one VM)
      create the machines and run the installer through cloud-init.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Air-gapped installs" id="air-gapped">
    <p>
      Each release ships a bundle with the images, the installer and offline docs. Copy it to the
      machine and run the installer from it; it notices there is no internet and loads the images
      from the bundle. Later updates work the same way: upload the new bundle on the Updates page.
      For AI, point Bemmoly at a model server on the same network.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Upgrades and rollback" id="upgrades">
    <CodeBlock code={UPGRADE} />
    <p>
      An upgrade takes a backup, pulls the new image and checks its signature, applies schema
      changes, restarts and waits for the health check. If the new version is not healthy within
      three minutes, it goes back to the previous one on its own. The Update button in the app runs
      the same steps; install with <code>--no-in-app-updates</code> to keep upgrades on the command
      line only.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rollback stays available for seven days after an upgrade, and it tells you what it keeps
      before it runs:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Code rollback</strong>, the usual case: the previous image starts against the same
        database. Nothing is lost, because every release works with the previous minor's schema.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Schema rollback</strong>: schema changes are undone first. Fields added since the
        upgrade are removed, and you see the list before confirming.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Restore rollback</strong>, only when a release made a change that cannot be undone:
        the backup from before the upgrade is restored. You see how many changes would be discarded,
        and can export them first.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Backups" id="backups">
    <p>
      Backups are part of the app. Choose the schedule, retention and destinations in Settings,
      under Storage and backups. By default a backup runs daily at 02:00 and keeps 7 daily, 4 weekly
      and 3 monthly copies on local disk; add any S3-compatible bucket as a second copy, encrypted
      before it leaves the machine.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each backup holds a database dump, the attachments added since the last one, and a manifest.
      Every dump is checked when written, and a weekly test restore proves the backups work.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>
        Keep a copy of <code>/var/bemmoly/.env</code> somewhere safe.
      </strong>{' '}
      It holds the key that decrypts stored provider keys and passwords, and it is not part of the
      backup. To move to a new machine: run the installer, copy <code>.env</code> and a backup
      across, and restore.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={BACKUP} />
  </Section>

  <Section title="Day to day" id="cli">
    <ul>
      {CLI.map(([command, what]) => (
        <li>
          <code>{command}</code>: {what}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Section>
</Page>
